  <data-orientation>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{ED9876C9-4C75-4C6E-A004-8CF16AA41A55}' />
      <desc>This visualization shows the number of pets in each pet type. There are 522 dogs, which is the largest group, while rabbits are the smallest with 493.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{46642F13-3699-475D-B3D0-392F2EEB024E}' />
      <desc>This visualization shows the adoption likelihood for pets with and without previous owners. Of the pets without a previous owner, 470 are likely to be adopted while 931 may not be. For pets that had a previous owner, 189 are likely to be adopted while 417 may not be.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{C1CC7B02-96E7-4282-A752-56CD291EFEB7}' />
      <desc>This visualization shows the number of pets in each size category. There are 663 small pets, 714 medium pets, and 630 large pets, so medium-sized pets are the most common in the shelter.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{030386A7-00E8-47A6-A0F9-71813C98C811}' />
      <desc>This visualization shows the number of pets in each color category. Looking at the data, white is the most common color among the pets, while brown is the least common.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{7673B5D3-790E-4798-AFCD-D63E047718A3}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares pet-size categories across different age groups to identify which combinations of age and size are most common within the shelter population. It seems the combinations that are the most common are medium pets who are 73 months and older followed by small pets of the same combination and large pets. </desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{D07BD68B-3BCE-4535-83E4-736CDE9D9FF4}' />
      <desc>This visualization shows the adoption likelihood for each pet size. Small pets have the most that are unlikely to be adopted with 546, followed by large pets with 533 and medium pets with 269. However, medium pets have the most that are likely to be adopted with 445, compared to 97 small pets and 117 large pets.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
    <data-orientation-item>
      <simple-id uuid='{A5DA2C6F-3410-4973-BD98-153B41F8D674}' />
      <desc>This visualization compares adoption outcomes between vaccinated and non-vaccinated pets. Looking at the data, 533 non-vaccinated pets are unlikely to be adopted, while 67 are likely to be adopted. Among the vaccinated pets, 592 are likely to be adopted, while 815 are unlikely to be adopted.</desc>
    </data-orientation-item>
  </data-orientation>
